{
  "schema": 1,
  "grade": "3",
  "band": "3-5",
  "focus": "Grade 3 is where vocabulary moves from naming things to naming qualities. Students already own a large store of concrete words; this year adds the precise adjectives, verbs, and nouns that let them describe a character's mood, a forest's floor, the way someone moved, or how big something really was. Units pair concrete-to-abstract character and nature words with the first systematic work on context clues, synonym and antonym ladders (said, walked, big, feel), simple prefixes and suffixes (un-, re-, dis-, mis-, pre-, -ful, -less, -ly, -er, -or, -ment, -tion), and multiple-meaning words. Disciplinary units introduce a few science and social studies terms and the verbs teachers use to ask for thinking. Compared with grade 2 the words are less concrete and the affix work is cumulative; compared with grade 4 there is no root work, no cause/effect signal language, and abstraction stays tied to pictures and stories.",
  "selection": "Candidates came from three questions. Which words appear across many grade 3-4 narrative and informational texts but are not yet part of speaking vocabulary (Tier 2, not the most frequent words, which students mostly know)? Which words let students say something they already understand more precisely (exhausted for tired, stroll for walk)? Which words carry productive word parts or cluster in a semantic field so that one unit teaches a web of related meanings? Shades-of-meaning units (size, speech, movement, feeling, light) use ladders so each word is taught against its neighbors. At most three Tier 3 words appear in a unit, and only in the three clearly named units for weather, social studies, and story terms. Every grade K-2 target already in the corpus was excluded, so common character, feeling, movement, and weather words that grades 1-2 teach were replaced by stronger neighbors. Deliberately left to later grades: Greek and Latin roots (grade 4), cause-and-effect signal words (grade 4), look-alike word pairs (grade 4), argument and evidence language (grade 5), and figurative-language terms such as simile and metaphor (grades 5-6).",
  "arc": [
